$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{4x^2-7x}{(\ln(x))^2}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[4x^2-7x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[(\ln(x))^2]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{8x-7}{2\ln(x)\cdot\dfrac{1}{x}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{8x^2-7x}{2\ln(x)}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[8x^2-7x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[2\ln(x)]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{16x-7}{\left(\dfrac{2}{x}\right)}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{16x^2-7x}{2}
\\\\
&=\infty
\end{align}$

Note that we used l’Hôpital’s rule *twice*, because the first time we used it, we ended with the indeterminate form $\dfrac{\infty}{\infty}$ too.

Also note that we were only able to use l’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[8x^2-7x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[2\ln(x)]}$ can actually be determined.
